# Client-only tracer overrides keyed by gun ID.
# Missing IDs use the original orange line tracer.
# Format: gunId style red green blue alpha length width
# Styles: none, line, laser, dot, dashed, beam, plasma, bolt,
# comet, pulse, rail, spark, needle, twin, zigzag, flare, streak, slug, blade
#
# COLUMN REFERENCE
# gunId  Weapon ID from the first column of guns.txt. Valid range: 0-127.
# style  Shape used to draw the tracer. Valid names are listed above.
# red    Amount of red in the tracer color. Range: 0-255.
# green  Amount of green in the tracer color. Range: 0-255.
# blue   Amount of blue in the tracer color. Range: 0-255.
# alpha  Tracer opacity/intensity. 0 is invisible; 255 is fully visible.
# length Trail length in world pixels. 0 uses the original speed-scaled length.
# width  Line/projectile thickness. Must be greater than 0; decimals are valid.
#
# RGB examples: 255 0 0 = red, 0 255 0 = green, 0 0 255 = blue,
# 255 255 255 = white, and 255 165 0 = the original orange.
# The active test assignments at the top give every current weapon a style.
#
# HOW TO ATTACH A TRACER TO A WEAPON
# 1. Find the weapon ID in data/guns.txt (the first value in its row).
# 2. Add one tracer row below using that same ID.
# 3. Do not start the row with #; # makes a row a comment and disables it.
# 4. Restart the game after saving this file.
# Example: AK47 is ID 18 in guns.txt. This active row would give it a red laser:
# 18 laser 255 30 30 255 64.0 1.0
# Only one active row should be used per gun ID. Guns without a row keep the
# original orange tracer. This file affects client graphics only, not damage,
# bullet speed, collision, server behavior, or network compatibility.
#
# STYLE REFERENCE
# none    Draws no tracer. Color, alpha, length, and width are ignored.
# line    Draws the original layered tracer: a bright short front fading into
#         a darker, longer tail. Both normal and additive blending are used.
# laser   Draws one solid colored line over a wide, dim additive glow.
# dot     Draws only a square projectile point. Length is ignored; width sets
#         its half-size, with a minimum value of 1 pixel.
# dashed  Draws three separated line segments along the configured length.
# beam    Draws a broad colored glow, a colored core, and a thin white center.
# plasma  Draws a short glowing trail ending in a large square energy head.
# bolt    Draws a solid shaft with two angled lines forming an arrowhead.
# comet   Draws a dim long tail, a brighter thick inner tail, and a square head.
# pulse   Draws five separated glowing squares that fade toward the rear.
# rail    Draws two parallel colored rails with a thin white center line.
# spark   Draws a short trail ending in a horizontal and vertical cross flash.
# needle  Draws an extra-thin line using only 35 percent of the chosen length.
# twin    Draws two solid parallel lines separated according to width.
# zigzag  Draws a five-segment additive electric path alternating side to side.
# flare   Draws a short thick trail ending in an eight-point star-shaped flash.
# streak  Draws a dim full-length tail with a brighter, thicker short front.
# slug    Draws a very short thick dark body with a thin bright center line.
# blade   Draws a spinning four-edged razor disc with a short motion trail.
#
# STYLE EXAMPLE ROWS (commented out and safe to copy)
# 25 none 255 255 255 0 0.0 1.0
# 17 line 255 180 40 225 28.0 1.0
# 23 laser 255 30 30 255 64.0 1.0
# 1 dot 255 210 120 255 0.0 1.5
# 18 dashed 255 200 70 230 72.0 1.5
# 36 beam 70 180 255 240 80.0 1.5
# 37 plasma 80 255 190 220 48.0 1.5
